Rem ==================================================================
Rem seedbooltest.shoddy — test program for seedbool.shoddy
Rem
Rem Assert-driven, in the seedregextest / seedengtest pattern: it prints
Rem what it is doing and stops the run at the first assertion that
Rem fails, ending with ALL ASSERTIONS PASSED when it does not.
Rem
Rem WHAT IS GRADED HERE IS THE BRIDGE AND NOT THE MACHINE.
Rem tst/bool.shoddy grades bool.shoddy — every identity, every code,
Rem every width rule. This file grades the glue between that machine and
Rem a keyboard: does the right cell come back, does a wrong cell refuse
Rem instead of aborting, and does the session survive it.
Rem
Rem WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL. seed-bool's coverage lived in
Rem tst/seedtest.shoddy, which walks guards across thirteen seeds and is
Rem deliberately not a catalogue. That was proportionate at twenty words.
Rem This seed is now forty-six, and the families it grew — the session
Rem width, two's complement, the codes, minimisation as text — each have
Rem behaviour that a guard walk does not reach.
Rem
Rem THE SESSION WIDTH IS THE THING TO GET WRONG. Fourteen words read it
Rem rather than taking it, so a test that never sets it grades one
Rem column of the table. Every width-reading word below is asserted at
Rem the default 32 AND at a narrow width where the answer differs, since
Rem an implementation that ignored the mode entirely would pass the
Rem first of those and fail the second.
Rem
Rem SURVIVAL IS PROVED BY THE NEXT LINE, never by the refusal. Refuses
Rem asserts three things at once: the line was refused, the stack is
Rem untouched by it, and a further line afterwards answers exactly what
Rem it would have answered if the bad line had never been typed. A
Rem refusal that quietly ate a cell passes the first and fails the third.
Rem
Rem THE TWO 53s ARE ASSERTED TO AGREE. reckoner owns the session width
Rem and states its own ceiling; bool owns the bit words and states one
Rem too. Neither machine includes the other to get it — the number comes
Rem from the same fact about a double either way — so the correspondence
Rem is pinned here, where both are in scope, rather than by a dependency.
Rem
Rem Nothing here touches a file, a socket or a window.
Rem
Rem Run: bin\mill run tst\seedbooltest.shoddy    (from the repo root)
Rem ==================================================================

Include "../machines/bool.shoddy"
Include "../machines/cuttle.shoddy"
Include "../machines/reckoner.shoddy"
Include "../machines/seq.shoddy"
Include "../machines/str.shoddy"
Include "../machines/seeds/seedbool.shoddy"
Include "../machines/seeds/seedseq.shoddy"

Rem seed-seq is folded in as well as seed-bool, and for one reason:
Rem LOWBIT's whole design is that its answer COMPOSES, and the words it
Rem composes with — LENGTH and FIRST — are seed-seq's. Asserting "{ }"
Rem renders correctly would grade the rendering; asserting
Rem "0 LOWBIT LENGTH" is 0 grades the design. The word-count assertions
Rem below filter on the seed-bool group, so the extra fold changes
Rem nothing they measure.
Def Base() As RckState
    RckSeedBool(RckSeedSeq(RckNew()))

Rem ---- reading an answer ----------------------------------------------

Def Ran(st As RckState, line As String) As RckState
    Select Case RckEval(st, line)
        Case RckNext(st2, out)
            st2
        Case RckStop(st2, why, out)
            st2
        Case Else
            st

Def Bad(st As RckState, line As String) As Boolean
    Select Case RckEval(st, line)
        Case RckStop(st2, why, out)
            True
        Case Else
            False

Def Why(st As RckState, line As String) As String
    Select Case RckEval(st, line)
        Case RckStop(st2, why, out)
            why
        Case Else
            ""

Def Text(st As RckState) As String
    Join(Map(CutCells(RckStack(st)), Fn(c) => CutShow(c, RckFmt(st))), " | ")

Rem The top cell's own text, unquoted — what a multi-line rendering needs,
Rem since CutShow would wrap it in quote marks and hide the newlines.
Def TopStr(st As RckState) As String
    Select Case First(CutCells(RckStack(st)))
        Case CutStr(s)
            s
        Case Else
            "NOT A STRING"

Rem What one line leaves on an empty stack at the default width.
Def Said(line As String) As String
    Text(Ran(Base(), line))

Rem The same, at a stated session width.
Def SaidAt(w As Number, line As String) As String
    Text(Ran(Ran(Base(), Str(w) & " WIDTH"), line))

Def StrAt(w As Number, line As String) As String
    TopStr(Ran(Ran(Base(), Str(w) & " WIDTH"), line))

Rem Which group a word was registered under — WIDTH's is "core", and
Rem that is the assertion, not a detail.
Def SeedOf(st As RckState, name As String) As String
    Select Case RckFind(st, name)
        Case Some(e)
            WordSeed(e)
        Case Else
            "NOT A WORD"

Rem Refused, with the reason naming what is wrong; the stack untouched;
Rem and a further line afterwards answering as though nothing happened.
Def Refuses(st As RckState, line As String, part As String) As Boolean
    Bad(st, line) And Instr(Why(st, line), part) > 0 And Text(Ran(st, line)) = Text(st) And Text(Ran(Ran(st, line), "1 1 +")) = Text(Ran(st, "1 1 +"))

Def Qm() As String
    RckQuoteMark()

Def Q(s As String) As String
    Qm() & s & Qm()

Rem Every character of s inside printable ASCII, which is what "survives
Rem being written down" means where file I/O is Latin-1.
Def AllAscii(s As String) As Boolean
    All(Range(1, Len(s)), Fn(i) => Asc(Mid(s, i, 1)) >= 32 And Asc(Mid(s, i, 1)) <= 126)

Def Main()
    Let s0 = Base()
    Rem A session at eight bits, which is where most of the width work
    Rem below is graded because a byte is small enough to read by eye.
    Let s8 = Ran(s0, "8 WIDTH")

    Print("--- the words are registered, with their metadata ---")
    Let mine = Filter(RckDict(s0), Fn(e) => WordSeed(e) = "seed-bool")
    Assert(Length(mine) = 46, "FORTY-SIX WORDS UNDER SEED-BOOL, AND NOT A FORTY-SEVENTH")
    Assert(IsEmpty(Filter(mine, Fn(e) => WordEffect(e) = "" Or WordDesc(e) = "")), "NOT ONE OF THEM IS WITHOUT AN EFFECT LINE AND A DESCRIPTION")
    Assert(All(mine, Fn(e) => Not RckTouches(e)), "EVERY WORD HERE IS PURE — READING A MODE IS NOT TOUCHING THE WORLD")
    Assert(RckKnows(s0, "ROTL") And RckKnows(s0, "ROTR") And RckKnows(s0, "SHRA"), "THE SHIFT FAMILY IS COMPLETE")
    Assert(RckKnows(s0, "SIGNED") And RckKnows(s0, "UNSIGNED") And RckKnows(s0, "SIGNEXTEND") And RckKnows(s0, "NEGW"), "AND THE TWO'S-COMPLEMENT FAMILY")
    Assert(RckKnows(s0, "ADDW") And RckKnows(s0, "SUBW") And RckKnows(s0, "MULW") And RckKnows(s0, "CARRIES") And RckKnows(s0, "OVERFLOWS"), "AND THE WRAPPING ARITHMETIC WITH BOTH ITS FLAGS")
    Assert(RckKnows(s0, "GRAY") And RckKnows(s0, "FROMGRAY") And RckKnows(s0, "BCD") And RckKnows(s0, "FROMBCD"), "AND THE CODES")
    Assert(RckKnows(s0, "PARITY") And RckKnows(s0, "PARITYBIT") And RckKnows(s0, "HAMMING"), "AND THE REST OF THEM")
    Assert(RckKnows(s0, "MINTERMS") And RckKnows(s0, "IMPLICANTS") And RckKnows(s0, "TABLE"), "AND MINIMISATION, WHICH USED TO BE OUT")
    Assert(RckKnows(s0, "LOWBIT") And RckKnows(s0, "HIGHBIT") And RckKnows(s0, "CARD") And RckKnows(s0, "CARDWITH"), "AND THE FOUR SMALL UNLOCKS")

    Print("--- WIDTH is the core's, not this seed's ---")
    Rem It belongs to the engine for the same reason the display format
    Rem does: several seeds will read it, and a mode owned by one domain's
    Rem seed is a mode the next domain cannot see.
    Assert(RckKnows(s0, "WIDTH") And RckKnows(s0, "WIDTH?"), "BOTH WIDTH WORDS ARE IN THE DICTIONARY")
    Assert(SeedOf(s0, "WIDTH") = "core" And SeedOf(s0, "WIDTH?") = "core", "AND BOTH BELONG TO THE CORE")
    Assert(Said("WIDTH?") = "32", "A FRESH SESSION IS THIRTY-TWO BITS WIDE")
    Assert(BoolBits() = 32, "WHICH IS THE SAME THIRTY-TWO BOOL NAMES, REACHED WITHOUT EITHER MACHINE INCLUDING THE OTHER")
    Assert(RckMaxWidth() = BoolMaxWidth(), "AND THE TWO CEILINGS AGREE AT 53, WHICH IS THE FACT ABOUT A DOUBLE BOTH OF THEM READ")
    Assert(Said("8 WIDTH WIDTH?") = "8", "WIDTH SETS IT AND WIDTH? REPORTS IT")
    Assert(Said("53 WIDTH WIDTH?") = "53", "THE TOP OF THE RANGE IS ALLOWED")
    Assert(Said("1 WIDTH WIDTH?") = "1", "AND SO IS THE BOTTOM")
    Assert(Said("8 WIDTH") = "", "SETTING IT PUSHES NOTHING, EXACTLY AS FIX AND DEG DO")

    Print("--- WIDTH refuses, and the session survives every refusal ---")
    Assert(Refuses(s0, "0 WIDTH", "WIDTH needs a width from 1 to 53, got 0"), "A WIDTH OF ZERO")
    Assert(Refuses(s0, "54 WIDTH", "WIDTH needs a width from 1 to 53, got 54"), "AND ONE PAST THE CEILING")
    Assert(Refuses(s0, "8.5 WIDTH", "WIDTH needs a whole number of 0 or more, got 8.5"), "AND A FRACTIONAL ONE")
    Assert(Refuses(s0, "0 1 - WIDTH", "WIDTH needs a whole number of 0 or more, got -1"), "AND A NEGATIVE ONE")
    Rem A refused WIDTH must leave the mode as it was, not half-set it.
    Assert(Text(Ran(Ran(s8, "99 WIDTH"), "WIDTH?")) = "8", "AND A REFUSED WIDTH LEAVES THE MODE WHERE IT WAS")

    Print("--- the width does not touch how numbers are shown ---")
    Rem R1.5: the render is FIX/SCI/STD's business. Two modes changing it
    Rem would be two modes fighting.
    Assert(SaidAt(8, "255") = "255", "AT EIGHT BITS 255 IS STILL SHOWN AS 255, NOT AS ITS BITS")

    Print("--- SHL reads the width now, and SHR still needs none ---")
    Assert(Said("1 7 SHL") = "128", "SHL AT THE DEFAULT WIDTH")
    Assert(SaidAt(8, "1 7 SHL") = "128", "AND TO THE TOP OF A BYTE AT EIGHT")
    Assert(SaidAt(8, "1 8 SHL") = "0", "ONE PLACE FURTHER AND THE BIT FALLS OFF THE END OF THE BYTE")
    Assert(Said("1 8 SHL") = "256", "WHILE AT THIRTY-TWO IT IS STILL THERE — WHICH IS THE MODE DOING SOMETHING")
    Rem SHR is untouched: a logical right shift cannot produce a bit the
    Rem input did not have, so there is no end for its answer to depend on.
    Assert(Said("256 4 SHR") = "16", "SHR NEEDS NO WIDTH AND TAKES NONE")
    Assert(SaidAt(8, "255 4 SHR") = "15", "AND ANSWERS THE SAME WHATEVER THE WIDTH IS")
    Assert(Refuses(s0, "1 99 SHL", "SHL needs a shift from 0 to 53, got 99"), "A SHIFT PAST THE CEILING IS STILL NAMED")
    Assert(Refuses(s8, "256 1 SHL", "SHL needs a value that fits 8 bits, and 256 does not"), "AND A VALUE THAT DOES NOT FIT THE SESSION WIDTH")

    Print("--- BITNOT reads the width too, so no word here asks for one ---")
    Rem It held out longest and was the worst one to leave: four of its
    Rem neighbours read the mode and it did not, which made it the one a
    Rem session would type wrong.
    Assert(SaidAt(8, "255 BITNOT") = "0", "EVERY BIT OF A FULL BYTE FLIPS TO NOTHING")
    Assert(SaidAt(8, "0 BITNOT") = "255", "AND NOTHING FLIPS TO EVERY BIT")
    Assert(SaidAt(8, "170 BITNOT") = "85", "10101010 COMPLEMENTS TO 01010101")
    Assert(Said("255 BITNOT") = "4294967040", "THE SAME VALUE AT THIRTY-TWO BITS FLIPS TWENTY-FOUR MORE OF THEM, WHICH IS WHY A COMPLEMENT NEEDS A WIDTH AT ALL")
    Assert(SaidAt(8, "255 BITNOT BITNOT") = "255", "AND COMPLEMENTING TWICE COMES HOME")
    Assert(Refuses(s8, "256 BITNOT", "BITNOT needs a value that fits 8 bits, and 256 does not"), "A VALUE TOO WIDE FOR THE SESSION")
    Rem The width bound moved to WIDTH, which is where it is now checked
    Rem once for every word rather than by each word for itself.
    Assert(Refuses(s0, "0 1 - BITNOT", "BITNOT needs a whole number of 0 or more, got -1"), "AND A NEGATIVE PATTERN")

    Print("--- ROTL, ROTR, SHRA ---")
    Assert(SaidAt(8, "1 1 ROTR") = "128", "ROTATING ONE RIGHT AT EIGHT BITS BRINGS THE BIT BACK AT THE TOP")
    Assert(SaidAt(8, "128 1 ROTL") = "1", "AND ROTATING THE TOP BIT LEFT BRINGS IT BACK AT THE BOTTOM")
    Assert(SaidAt(8, "1 8 ROTL") = "1", "A FULL TURN IS THE IDENTITY")
    Assert(Said("1 1 ROTR") = "2147483648", "THE SAME ROTATE AT THIRTY-TWO LANDS SOMEWHERE ELSE ENTIRELY")
    Rem SHRA copies the sign bit down where SHR brings in zeros, which is
    Rem the whole difference between the two and the reason both exist.
    Assert(SaidAt(8, "128 1 SHRA") = "192", "AN ARITHMETIC SHIFT COPIES THE TOP BIT DOWN")
    Assert(SaidAt(8, "128 1 SHR") = "64", "WHERE A LOGICAL ONE BRINGS IN A ZERO")
    Assert(SaidAt(8, "64 1 SHRA") = "32", "AND A CLEAR TOP BIT SHIFTS LIKE ANY OTHER")
    Assert(Refuses(s8, "300 1 ROTL", "ROTL needs a value that fits 8 bits, and 300 does not"), "ROTL REFUSES A VALUE TOO WIDE FOR THE SESSION")
    Assert(Refuses(s8, "1 60 ROTR", "ROTR needs a shift from 0 to 53, got 60"), "AND ROTR A COUNT PAST THE CEILING")
    Assert(Refuses(s8, "0 1 - 1 SHRA", "SHRA needs a whole number of 0 or more, got -1"), "AND SHRA A NEGATIVE PATTERN")

    Print("--- SIGNED and UNSIGNED, the pair that reads a pattern as a number ---")
    Assert(SaidAt(8, "255 SIGNED") = "-1", "255 AT EIGHT BITS IS MINUS ONE")
    Assert(Said("255 SIGNED") = "255", "AND AT THIRTY-TWO IT IS STILL 255 — THE SAME BITS, A DIFFERENT WORD LENGTH")
    Assert(SaidAt(8, "128 SIGNED") = "-128", "THE MOST NEGATIVE VALUE A BYTE HOLDS")
    Assert(SaidAt(8, "127 SIGNED") = "127", "AND THE MOST POSITIVE")
    Rem UNSIGNED is the one word here whose input may be negative, which
    Rem is exactly what it is for.
    Assert(SaidAt(8, "0 1 - UNSIGNED") = "255", "MINUS ONE AT EIGHT BITS IS THE PATTERN 255")
    Assert(SaidAt(8, "0 128 - UNSIGNED") = "128", "AND MINUS 128 IS 128")
    Assert(SaidAt(8, "127 UNSIGNED") = "127", "A POSITIVE VALUE IN RANGE PASSES STRAIGHT THROUGH")
    Assert(SaidAt(8, "255 SIGNED UNSIGNED") = "255", "AND THE ROUND TRIP THROUGH BOTH COMES HOME")
    Assert(Refuses(s8, "128 UNSIGNED", "UNSIGNED needs a value from -128 to 127 at 8 signed bits"), "128 IS NOT A SIGNED BYTE, AND THE REFUSAL SAYS WHICH RANGE IT MISSED")
    Assert(Refuses(s8, "0 129 - UNSIGNED", "UNSIGNED needs a value from -128 to 127 at 8 signed bits"), "AND NEITHER IS MINUS 129")
    Assert(Refuses(s8, "1.5 UNSIGNED", "UNSIGNED needs a whole number, got 1.5"), "AND A FRACTION IS NOT A BIT PATTERN AT ALL")

    Print("--- SIGNEXTEND widens, and refuses to narrow ---")
    Assert(Text(Ran(Ran(s0, "16 WIDTH"), "255 8 SIGNEXTEND")) = "65535", "A NEGATIVE BYTE WIDENED TO SIXTEEN BITS KEEPS ITS SIGN")
    Assert(Text(Ran(Ran(s0, "16 WIDTH"), "127 8 SIGNEXTEND")) = "127", "AND A POSITIVE ONE IS UNCHANGED")
    Assert(Refuses(s8, "255 16 SIGNEXTEND", "SIGNEXTEND cannot widen from 16 bits to the session width of 8"), "WIDENING DOWNWARDS IS NOT WIDENING, AND IS NAMED AS SUCH")

    Print("--- NEGW, and the zero it gets right ---")
    Assert(SaidAt(8, "1 NEGW") = "255", "MINUS ONE AS A BYTE PATTERN")
    Assert(SaidAt(8, "0 NEGW") = "0", "AND ZERO NEGATES TO ZERO, WHICH IS THE CASE A NOT-PLUS-ONE IMPLEMENTATION GETS WRONG")
    Assert(SaidAt(8, "128 NEGW") = "128", "THE MOST NEGATIVE VALUE NEGATES TO ITSELF")

    Print("--- the wrapping arithmetic, and both of its flags ---")
    Assert(SaidAt(8, "200 100 ADDW") = "44", "200 PLUS 100 IN A BYTE IS 44")
    Assert(Said("200 100 ADDW") = "300", "AND AT THIRTY-TWO IT IS JUST 300")
    Assert(SaidAt(8, "10 20 SUBW") = "246", "BELOW ZERO COMES BACK ROUND THE TOP")
    Assert(SaidAt(8, "20 10 SUBW") = "10", "AND AN ORDINARY SUBTRACTION IS ORDINARY")
    Assert(SaidAt(8, "16 16 MULW") = "0", "256 IN A BYTE IS NOTHING BUT THE BITS THAT FIT")
    Assert(SaidAt(8, "15 15 MULW") = "225", "AND A PRODUCT THAT FITS IS ITSELF")
    Rem CARRIES and OVERFLOWS are different questions with different
    Rem answers, which is the reason a machine giving the arithmetic
    Rem without the flags would be teaching half of it.
    Assert(SaidAt(8, "200 100 CARRIES") = "True", "200 PLUS 100 CARRIES OUT OF A BYTE")
    Assert(SaidAt(8, "200 100 OVERFLOWS") = "False", "AND AS SIGNED VALUES — MINUS 56 PLUS 100 — IT DOES NOT OVERFLOW")
    Assert(SaidAt(8, "100 100 CARRIES") = "False", "100 PLUS 100 DOES NOT CARRY")
    Assert(SaidAt(8, "100 100 OVERFLOWS") = "True", "AND YET IT DOES OVERFLOW AS SIGNED, WHICH IS WHY BOTH WORDS ARE HERE")
    Assert(Refuses(s8, "256 1 ADDW", "ADDW needs a value that fits 8 bits, and 256 does not"), "A FIRST OPERAND TOO WIDE")
    Assert(Refuses(s8, "1 256 SUBW", "SUBW needs a value that fits 8 bits, and 256 does not"), "AND A SECOND ONE")
    Assert(Refuses(s8, "300 1 CARRIES", "CARRIES needs a value that fits 8 bits, and 300 does not"), "AND THE FLAGS GUARD THE SAME WAY")
    Assert(Refuses(s8, "1 300 OVERFLOWS", "OVERFLOWS needs a value that fits 8 bits, and 300 does not"), "BOTH OF THEM")

    Print("--- LOWBIT and HIGHBIT: absence is an empty list ---")
    Rem The whole answer to the old exclusion. None() has nowhere to go on
    Rem a stack; { } has. One cell always, so UNDO's accounting holds.
    Assert(Said("12 LOWBIT") = "{ 2 }", "THE LOWEST SET BIT OF 12 IS BIT 2")
    Assert(Said("12 HIGHBIT") = "{ 3 }", "AND THE HIGHEST IS BIT 3")
    Assert(Said("0 LOWBIT") = "{ }", "ZERO HAS NO LOWEST SET BIT, AND SAYS SO AS AN EMPTY LIST RATHER THAN REFUSING")
    Assert(Said("0 HIGHBIT") = "{ }", "AND NO HIGHEST ONE EITHER")
    Assert(Said("1 LOWBIT") = "{ 0 }", "BIT ZERO IS AN INDEX AND NOT AN ABSENCE, WHICH IS THE CASE A -1 SENTINEL CONFUSES")
    Rem The composition the design is for: LENGTH turns the answer into
    Rem the question "was there a bit at all?" in words the keyboard has.
    Assert(Said("0 LOWBIT LENGTH") = "0", "LENGTH OF THE EMPTY ANSWER IS ZERO")
    Assert(Said("12 LOWBIT LENGTH") = "1", "AND OF A FOUND ONE IS ONE")
    Assert(Said("12 LOWBIT FIRST") = "2", "AND FIRST TAKES THE INDEX OUT")
    Rem One cell, always — the rule RXGROUPS keeps for the same reason.
    Assert(CutDepth(RckStack(Ran(s0, "0 LOWBIT"))) = 1, "THE EMPTY ANSWER IS STILL ONE CELL, SO UNDO'S ACCOUNTING HOLDS")
    Assert(CutDepth(RckStack(Ran(s0, "12 HIGHBIT"))) = 1, "AND SO IS A FOUND ONE")
    Assert(Refuses(s0, "0 1 - LOWBIT", "LOWBIT needs a whole number of 0 or more, got -1"), "A NEGATIVE STILL REFUSES — ABSENCE IS NOT THE SAME AS NONSENSE")

    Print("--- CARD, and the ASCII that survives being written down ---")
    Assert(StrAt(8, "10 CARD") = "....#.#.", "TEN AS A ROW OF EIGHT HOLES")
    Assert(Len(StrAt(8, "10 CARD")) = 8, "ONE CHARACTER PER BIT OF THE SESSION WIDTH")
    Assert(AllAscii(StrAt(8, "10 CARD")), "AND EVERY ONE OF THEM PLAIN ASCII, WHICH IS THE WHOLE OF THE OLD EXCLUSION")
    Assert(Len(StrAt(16, "10 CARD")) = 16, "A WIDER SESSION IS A WIDER CARD")
    Assert(StrAt(8, "255 CARD") = "########", "EVERY BIT SET")
    Assert(StrAt(8, "0 CARD") = "........", "AND NONE")
    Assert(StrAt(8, "10 " & Q("*") & " " & Q("-") & " CARDWITH") = "----*-*-", "CARDWITH TAKES ITS OWN GLYPHS")
    Assert(Refuses(s8, "256 CARD", "CARD needs a value that fits 8 bits, and 256 does not"), "A VALUE TOO WIDE FOR THE CARD")
    Assert(Refuses(s8, "10 1 " & Q("-") & " CARDWITH", "CARDWITH needs a STRING, got NUMBER"), "AND A GLYPH THAT IS NOT TEXT")

    Print("--- the codes ---")
    Assert(Said("5 GRAY") = "7", "FIVE IN GRAY CODE IS SEVEN")
    Assert(Said("7 FROMGRAY") = "5", "AND BACK AGAIN")
    Assert(Said("5 GRAY FROMGRAY") = "5", "THE ROUND TRIP THROUGH BOTH")
    Rem The property that makes Gray code worth having: consecutive values
    Rem differ in exactly one bit, which HAMMING is right here to measure.
    Assert(Said("5 GRAY 6 GRAY HAMMING") = "1", "CONSECUTIVE VALUES DIFFER IN EXACTLY ONE BIT, WHICH IS WHAT GRAY CODE IS FOR")
    Assert(Said("12 10 HAMMING") = "2", "HAMMING COUNTS THE POSITIONS TWO PATTERNS DIFFER IN")
    Assert(Said("255 255 HAMMING") = "0", "AND IS ZERO FOR A PATTERN AGAINST ITSELF")
    Assert(Said("7 PARITY") = "True", "THREE BITS SET IS ODD PARITY")
    Assert(Said("3 PARITY") = "False", "AND TWO IS EVEN")
    Assert(Said("7 PARITYBIT") = "1", "THE EVEN-PARITY BIT TO APPEND TO AN ODD COUNT IS ONE")
    Assert(Said("3 PARITYBIT") = "0", "AND TO AN EVEN COUNT IS ZERO")
    Assert(Said("42 BCD") = "66", "FORTY-TWO PACKED AS BCD IS 0x42, WHICH IS 66")
    Assert(Said("66 FROMBCD") = "42", "AND READ BACK IS FORTY-TWO AGAIN")
    Assert(Said("1234 BCD FROMBCD") = "1234", "THE ROUND TRIP THROUGH BOTH")
    Assert(Refuses(s0, "10 FROMBCD", "FROMBCD: '10' HAS THE NIBBLE 10, WHICH IS NOT A DECIMAL DIGIT"), "A NIBBLE ABOVE NINE IS NOT A DECIMAL DIGIT, AND THE REFUSAL NAMES FROMBCD AND NOT BOOLFROMBCD")
    Assert(Refuses(s0, "10000000000000 BCD", "BCD packs at most thirteen digits"), "AND A FOURTEENTH DIGIT DOES NOT FIT 53 BITS")
    Assert(Refuses(s0, "0 1 - GRAY", "GRAY needs a whole number of 0 or more, got -1"), "A NEGATIVE HAS NO GRAY CODE")
    Assert(Refuses(s0, "1.5 PARITY", "PARITY needs a whole number of 0 or more, got 1.5"), "AND A FRACTION HAS NO BITS TO COUNT")

    Print("--- minimisation and truth tables, as text ---")
    Rem The product term never reaches the stack: bool renders it and a
    Rem string is a Cell, which is the whole of that old exclusion.
    Assert(StrAt(32, "{ 0 1 2 3 } 3 MINTERMS") = "A'", "MINTERMS 0..3 OVER THREE VARIABLES IS EXACTLY A-NOT, WHICH HAS ONE ANSWER AND SO CAN BE ASSERTED LITERALLY")
    Rem R8.5: the cover step is greedy and the result is minimal in
    Rem practice rather than proven minimal, so a literal string here would
    Rem be asserting which of several equally valid covers the greedy step
    Rem happened to pick. The bound is what the requirement states.
    Let sop = StrAt(32, "{ 0 1 2 5 6 7 } 3 MINTERMS")
    Assert(Length(Split(sop, " + ")) <= 3, "SIX MINTERMS WITH NO ESSENTIAL PRIME IMPLICANT COVER IN AT MOST THREE TERMS")
    Assert(Len(sop) > 0, "AND THE ANSWER IS A RENDERING AND NOT AN EMPTY STRING")
    Assert(StrAt(32, "{ } 3 MINTERMS") = "0", "THE EMPTY MINTERM SET IS THE CONSTANT FALSE")
    Rem IMPLICANTS is the chart behind MINTERMS rather than one cover of
    Rem it, so it never has fewer terms than the cover does.
    Assert(Length(Split(StrAt(32, "{ 0 1 2 5 6 7 } 3 IMPLICANTS"), " + ")) >= Length(Split(sop, " + ")), "THE PRIME IMPLICANTS ARE NEVER FEWER THAN THE COVER CHOSEN FROM THEM")
    Rem The table is one string of 2^n rows under a header, so a caller
    Rem gets a screen and not a wall.
    Let tbl = StrAt(32, "{ 1 2 } 2 TABLE")
    Assert(Length(Split(tbl, Chr(10))) = 5, "TWO VARIABLES IS FOUR ROWS UNDER ONE HEADER LINE")
    Assert(StartsWith(tbl, "A B | OUT"), "AND THE HEADER NAMES THE VARIABLES THE SEED CHOSE FOR IT")
    Assert(Instr(tbl, "F T | T") > 0, "THE ROW FOR MINTERM 1 IS TRUE")
    Assert(Instr(tbl, "F F | F") > 0, "AND THE ROW FOR MINTERM 0, WHICH WAS NOT ASKED FOR, IS FALSE")
    Rem n is a variable count and not the session width: letting WIDTH
    Rem govern a truth table would be one mode answering a question nobody
    Rem pointed it at.
    Assert(Length(Split(StrAt(8, "{ 1 2 } 2 TABLE"), Chr(10))) = 5, "A NARROW SESSION DOES NOT NARROW A TRUTH TABLE — THE WIDTH IS NOT THE VARIABLE COUNT")
    Assert(Refuses(s0, "{ 0 1 } 13 MINTERMS", "MINTERMS needs a variable count from 1 to 12, got 13"), "THIRTEEN VARIABLES IS A HANG AND NOT A SLOW ANSWER, SO IT IS REFUSED BY NAME")
    Assert(Refuses(s0, "{ 0 8 } 3 MINTERMS", "MINTERMS needs every minterm from 0 to 7 for 3 variables"), "AND A MINTERM OUTSIDE THE VARIABLE COUNT")
    Assert(Refuses(s0, "5 3 TABLE", "TABLE needs a LIST, got NUMBER"), "AND MINTERMS THAT ARE NOT A LIST AT ALL")
    Assert(Refuses(s0, "{ 0 1.5 } 3 IMPLICANTS", "IMPLICANTS needs a whole number of 0 or more, got 1.5"), "AND A MINTERM THAT IS NOT A WHOLE NUMBER")

    Print("--- the twenty that were here first still answer as they did ---")
    Rem Only SHL changed, and it changed deliberately. Everything else is
    Rem asserted unchanged so that a regression in the new work cannot hide
    Rem behind a suite that only grades the new work.
    Assert(Said("255 HEX") = Q("FF"), "HEX")
    Assert(Said("5 BIN") = Q("101"), "BIN")
    Assert(Said("1295 36 INBASE") = Q("ZZ"), "INBASE AT THE TOP OF THE ALPHABET")
    Assert(Said(Q("ff") & " 16 FROMBASE") = "255", "FROMBASE, CASE-INSENSITIVE")
    Assert(Said("12 10 BITAND") = "8", "BITAND")
    Assert(Said("12 10 BITOR") = "14", "BITOR")
    Assert(Said("12 10 BITXOR") = "6", "BITXOR")
    Assert(SaidAt(8, "0 BITNOT") = "255", "BITNOT AT THE SESSION WIDTH")
    Assert(Said("8 MASK") = "255", "MASK")
    Assert(Said("171 7 4 GETFIELD") = "10", "GETFIELD")
    Assert(Said("5 0 BITAT") = "True", "BITAT")
    Assert(Said("7 POPCOUNT") = "3", "POPCOUNT")
    Assert(Said("TRUE FALSE NAND") = "True", "NAND")
    Assert(Said("TRUE FALSE XOR") = "True", "XOR")
    Assert(Said("TRUE FALSE IMPLIES") = "False", "IMPLIES")
    Assert(Said("TRUE TRUE IFF") = "True", "IFF")

    Print("--- a worked session: reading a byte four ways ---")
    Rem The shape somebody actually types, threaded through one state, to
    Rem prove the mode persists across lines rather than only within one.
    Let w = Ran(s0, "8 WIDTH")
    Assert(Text(Ran(w, "WIDTH?")) = "8", "THE WIDTH SET ON ONE LINE IS STILL THERE ON THE NEXT")
    Assert(TopStr(Ran(w, "202 CARD")) = "##..#.#.", "THE BYTE AS HOLES")
    Assert(Text(Ran(w, "202 SIGNED")) = "-54", "AS A SIGNED VALUE")
    Assert(Text(Ran(w, "202 HEX")) = Q("CA"), "IN HEXADECIMAL")
    Assert(Text(Ran(w, "202 HIGHBIT")) = "{ 7 }", "AND ITS TOP SET BIT")
    Assert(Text(Ran(w, "202 1 ROTL")) = "149", "ROTATED ONE LEFT INSIDE THE BYTE")

    Print("")
    Print("ALL ASSERTIONS PASSED")
